Here is the culprit:
When there is a discrepancy between `_tasks` and `_cluster/state`, the `TransportStopTransformAction` (which extends `TransportTasksAction`) **will not** call the `taskOperation` method as calling this method depends solely on `_tasks`.
See the following code snippet from `TransportTasksAction`:
```
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    protected List<OperationTask> processTasks(TasksRequest request) {
        if (request.getTargetTaskId().isSet()) {
            // we are only checking one task, we can optimize it
            Task task = taskManager.getTask(request.getTargetTaskId().getId());
            if (task != null) {
                if (request.match(task)) {
                    return List.of((OperationTask) task);
                } else {
                    throw new ResourceNotFoundException("task [{}] doesn't support this operation", request.getTargetTaskId());
                }
            } else {
                throw new ResourceNotFoundException("task [{}] is missing", request.getTargetTaskId());
            }
        } else {
            final var tasks = new ArrayList<OperationTask>();
            for (Task task : taskManager.getTasks().values()) {
                if (request.match(task)) {
                    tasks.add((OperationTask) task);
                }
            }
            return tasks;
        }
    }
```

Therefore, we should **not** send task removal request in `taskOperation` method, but in the `doExecute` method instead.
